  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Between Gourdon, Le Bar-sur-Loup and Tourettes-sur-Loup, these gorges are known for their waterfalls and fast-flowing water, in a spectacular setting in the Nice backcountry, well away from the coast's beach atmosphere. The Cascade de Courmes has been a listed historic site since 1913.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>A major safety point: swimming is officially and explicitly BANNED at the Cascade de Courmes, under a municipal order of 8 April 2003 that bans all sporting activity and swimming across the whole site (listed site, DREAL regulatory record). At the Saut du Loup too, entering the water is reported as strictly banned by a municipal sign. Access to the banks is largely private — only the path itself is public. Dogs are tolerated provided they are kept on a leash (Préalpes d'Azur Regional Nature Park). No parking is possible right at the Cascade de Courmes (roadside only); a car park exists at the Saut du Loup, along the gorge road at Gourdon.</p>
      <p>The Préalpes d'Azur Regional Nature Park describes the upper Gorges du Loup between the locality of Bramafan, on the D6, and the hamlet of Pont-du-Loup, in the commune of Tourrettes-sur-Loup. The valley is very steep-sided: the park specifies that there is hardly anywhere to stop, except at the Saut du Loup, where a car park has been built to visit this private site. The Saut du Loup is private property; a paid viewpoint overlooks the waterfalls. The Côte d'Azur tourist office states "dogs accepted but only on a leash", free access, all year subject to favourable weather conditions.</p>
      <p>The park's recommendations for the Natura 2000 site "Rivières et Gorges du Loup" are: no motorised traffic, no picking, no dumping of waste, no fires, dogs tolerated on a leash. In its guide to outdoor activities, the park also reminds visitors that this is a rural area where flocks, guard dogs and wildlife are sensitive to disturbance: keep your dog on a leash, away from flocks, and pick up its droppings. It is very busy in summer, roadside parking is difficult and partly banned (a barrier closed parking upstream of the tunnel in 2023): arrive early or leave the car in Gourdon.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the perched village of Gourdon, clinging to the cliff, offers a dizzying view over the gorges — a great stop to admire the site, but plan for a walk, not a swim, which is strictly banned here.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Saut du Loup, an iconic waterfall in the area, is a classic stop for visitors to the region (swimming also banned, see above).</p>
      <p>For walking, a loop of about 12.9 km starting from the Rouguière car park, in the centre of Gourdon, goes down the old mule track towards Pont-du-Loup and passes the Foulon aqueduct. The Visorando sheet recommends poles, hiking shoes, a yellow vest and a light for the second tunnel before the Saut du Loup, and reminds walkers to stay on the path, dogs on a leash. The park notes that the gorge path deteriorates in summer under trampling: stay on the waymarked route. The park also mentions the Lavandaie de Marie, which offers a path above the cliffs with views over the gorges.</p>
